-- A name with two meanings in one operand, and a WHERE constant in the next.
-- `size thing` has three readings that check (size at Colour with thing at
-- Colour; the polymorphic size with either thing), so it is ambiguous, and
-- both names are reported. The check of the expression runs the readings
-- that nothing after them can tell apart as one (legalese/l4-ide#683), and
-- the WHERE constant's own declaration is checked under a prune of its own.
-- That prune must count only its own outcomes: counting the merged outcome
-- of the operand before it as two again made the constant ambiguous on that
-- path, killed it, and left one reading, which the module then accepted.
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue
DECLARE Light IS ONE OF Red, Green

GIVETH A Colour
DECIDE thing IS Blue

GIVETH A Light
DECIDE thing IS Green

GIVEN x IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE size x IS 2

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
      x IS AN a
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE size x IS 1

GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE score IS (size thing) PLUS (z WHERE z MEANS 5)

#EVAL score
